WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.680
Clowns are the creepiest children's entertainers
on Earth, so naturally, they've been used

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.280
to terrify and delight grown-up film fans
for decades.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.120
From homicidal aliens to juggalo cowboys,
here are our picks for the four best - and

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.940
four worst - clown movies of all time.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.780
If you haven't seen Terrifier, congratulations
on your ability to sleep a full eight hours

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.400
without waking up in a cold sweat.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:24.540
An indie film that sat on the shelf for a
few years before being released, Terrifier

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:28.060
is one of those rare horror movies that doesn't
seem to feel the need to over-explain its

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:28.560
villain.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.460
The audience knows that he's a clown, and
he's probably demonic.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:32.480
But that's about it.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:37.200
He's silent, grinning, and more than delighted
to go on a trip to Slaughtertown any time

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:37.700
he can.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:42.240
Terrifier is a low-budget horror movie spinning
off of an even lower-budget short film, and

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.789
it's got "love letter to the good old days
of horror" dripping off of it like viscera

00:00:45.789 --> 00:00:47.500
off a clown monster's chainsaw.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.280
It has gory practical effects.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.340
It has party girls getting mutilated.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.720
And if you're down with slasher throwback
sickness, it's 100% worth looking up.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:00.080
Yes, 1990's TV version of It is charming in
it own way.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.640
It's a slice of horror filmmaking from a simpler
time.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.820
But it's just a bit agonizing to watch without
the rose-colored glasses of nostalgia.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.260
Don't get us wrong: Tim Curry's performance
as Pennywise is fantastic - it's the movie

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:16.080
around him, hampered by a made-for-TV budget
and content restrictions, that hasn't aged

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:16.580
well.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.420
There's the barely-animated giant spider at
the conclusion.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:23.300
There's the rough acting and the dramatic
screaming about Pennywise being too mean.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:27.810
There's the filmmakers' steadfast faith in
the usually outstanding Harry Anderson's comedic

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:32.180
stylings being able to carry the day, only
to watch him uncharacteristically drop the

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:32.680
ball.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:35.880
"Come on, Tozier, get a grip."

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:41.060
Just shy of three decades later, It holds
a place in pop culture history as quite possibly

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:44.610
being the reason that every other millennial
seems to be afraid of clowns.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.020
Watched with fresh eyes, though?

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.250
Not very scary at all.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:51.649
The words "so bad it's good" have been thrown
around so much that they've pretty much lost

00:01:51.649 --> 00:01:52.880
all meaning at this point.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:56.979
Cultural interpretations of what's camp and
what's just vanilla-flavored lameness ebb

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:57.860
and flow over time.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:01.640
Somewhere, floating atop the tides of our
willingness to accept silliness, there is

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.620
the unsinkable ship that is Killer Klowns
from Outer Space.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.120
Killer Klowns is a tough movie to sell on
paper.

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:11.920
No two ways about it, it's the story of a
species of alien invaders who look, act, and

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:12.820
accessorize like clowns.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.520
Their ships look like a circus tent and they
kill with an array of weapons, including pies.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:21.230
If that sounds like a good time to you, you're
in luck: There are rumblings of an upcoming

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:22.960
sequel from the original creators.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.970
John Wayne Gacy's list of real-life atrocities
is so long that we'll likely never know the

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:30.500
exact number of deaths for which he was responsible.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.720
When the details of Gacy's crimes are laid
out, most people are inclined to instinctively

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.120
recoil in horror and disgust - but a small
sliver of the world's population veers left

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.040
and says, "Now that's a picture!"

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:43.700
"I'm a very important man."

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:51.760
Released in 2003 under the names Gacy and
The Crawl Space, this true-crime depiction

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.379
of the life of John Wayne Gacy juxtaposes
his sadism against his gigs working as a clown

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:57.300
for local events.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.480
Exploitative, sensationalist, and featuring
Adam Baldwin from Firefly, it also stars the

00:03:02.489 --> 00:03:06.720
guy who played Pee-Wee's bully in Pee-Wee's
Big Adventure in the title role, so it just

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:07.720
might ruin your childhood.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.909
When it was first announced that Stephen King's
It was being re-adapted for the big screen,

00:03:12.909 --> 00:03:17.519
fans of the 1990 TV movie might have been
skeptical - but that lasted exactly as long

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.100
as it took for them to see Bill Skarsgård
in character.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:20.960
Yikes.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.260
Look: The new It movies aren't perfect.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:27.260
The CGI can be less than impressive, and there
are unsatisfying moments.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:31.659
But as adaptations of million-page books about
trans-dimensional ageless horrors go, they're

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:32.240
a hoot.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:37.040
They manage to pull believable performances
out of the child stars, which is sort of the

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:38.840
filmmaking equivalent of landing on Mars.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.320
And Bill Hader is a revelation, which came
as no surprise to fans of his masterful work

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:44.320
on HBO's Barry.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.200
"Maybe this is all I'm good at.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:47.320
I don't know."

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:53.519
1989's Clownhouse is a slasher in the same
vein as every other low-budget entry in the

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:54.620
genre from that era.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:59.100
It follows the story of three brothers being
stalked by a trio of escaped mental patients

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.300
dressed as clowns and lusting for blood.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:05.220
There are misunderstandings and mixups, as
tends to happen in these situations.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:10.360
At one point, a young Sam Rockwell - in his
film debut - gets stabbed into unconsciousness.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.280
The movie ends with a title card reading,
quote, "No man can hide from his fears; as

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.000
they are a part of him, they will always know
where he is hiding."

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:25.520
And you know what?

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.180
That would've been an okay ending to an unremarkable
and largely dull movie if it wasn't for the

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.100
real-world horror happening behind the scenes
on the set of the film.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:38.360
Director Victor Salva abused one of Clownhouse's
child stars, and was convicted and sentenced

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:42.740
to three years in prison - ultimately serving
less than half of his sentence before popping

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:45.700
back onto the scene to direct Powder in 1995.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:50.270
His career has continued with movies like
the Jeepers Creepers franchise, and a constant

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:53.520
low-grade public curiosity as to how this
guy still has a career.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:57.420
Cult filmmaker Bobcat Goldthwait is a national
treasure.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.520
Once a comedian known for his aggressively
annoying persona, he's become a director whose

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.260
movies tend to be tragic, complicated, peculiar,
excessive, and agonizing.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:10.540
Before World's Greatest Dad and God Bless
America, he wrote and directed a movie called

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:14.280
Shakes the Clown, and it's been dividing critics
and audiences ever since.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.210
The elevator pitch version is that it's the
story of an alcoholic birthday clown who's

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:22.910
framed for murder and has to outfox the borderline
Mad Max-level gangs of performers who rule

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:24.140
the entertainment circuit.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:27.880
The more complicated version is that it's
Goldthwait's representation of the comedy

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:30.920
cliques that exist at every open mic community
in America.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:32.780
Some critics called it amazing.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:33.980
Some called it terrible.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:38.520
Perhaps the highest praise came, believe it
or not, from Martin Scorsese, who called it

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.700
"the Citizen Kane of alcoholic clown movies."

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:41.540
"Okay.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:43.060
Salud!"

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:47.560
Over the years, it has become almost a rite
of passage for a successful band to dip their

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:48.900
toes into the world of Hollywood.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:51.560
The Beatles had classics like Help! and Yellow
Submarine.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:55.900
The Spice Girls irrevocably changed the geography
and political structure of the Earth when

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.800
they terraformed the planet into a newer,
better Spice World.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:03.880
And in 2010, a posse of chemically imbalanced
clowns proved once and for all that just because

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:06.740
you can't figure out magnets doesn't mean
you can't run a camera.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:12.240
Big Money Rustlas is the long-awaited follow-up
to 2001's Big Money Hustlas, the first Insane

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:13.280
Clown Posse movie.

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:16.820
It's set in the wild American West of 1837,
for some reason.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:20.520
Visually, it appears to have been shot on
a budget of whatever loose change was wedged

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:23.050
into the cushions of their Insane Clown couches.

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:26.160
The script seems to have been less "written"
and more "found somewhere."

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.160
Big Money Rustlas was pretty obviously a movie
made for the fans.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:34.060
It's goofy and weird, but if you don't care
at all about ICP, the whole thing is just

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.400
borderline unwatchable.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:36.840
"The name's Wolf.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:37.500
Sugar Wolf.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:42.820
As in AAAAAAAOOOOOOOWWWWWWWW!”

